Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) might be giants if they are distant; however, apparent magnitude alone is not sufficient since it depends on distance.  
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means the star is farther away; giants tend to be luminous and can be seen at larger distances with moderate brightness, so smaller Plx might indicate giants.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces confidence in distance measurement; less relevant directly to classification but important for data quality.  
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature; giants can have a wide range of B-V, but some spectral types correspond to typical B-V ranges.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III, III/IV) directly indicates whether a star is a giant (III) or not (V = main sequence). This is the strongest indicator.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have higher luminosity and thus lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes compared to main sequence stars of the same spectral type. Lower Amag (numerically smaller) suggests giants.  

