Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) could be giants since giants tend to be luminous, but apparent magnitude also depends on distance, so alone it is not decisive.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants can be both near and far, so this alone is not decisive, but combined with apparent magnitude it helps estimate absolute magnitude.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces confidence in distance and absolute magnitude estimates; less useful directly for classification.
- B-V (color index): Giants often have specific color indices corresponding to their spectral type and evolutionary stage; for example, giants tend to have larger B-V (redder) than main sequence stars of the same spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class part (e.g., V, III, IV) is the most direct indicator of giant status. Class III or III/IV indicates giant or subgiant stars; class V indicates main sequence (non-giant).
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants are intrinsically brighter, so they have lower (more negative or smaller) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of the same spectral type.

